origin/main:docs/issues/820/byok-research-brief.md:3:Scope: tenants paste their own third-party API keys; stored server-side; injected per-execution, including into sandboxes running untrusted tenant code. Two deploy modes (SaaS + self-host). This brief is the MANDATORY grounding for the BYOK secret-vault plan — cite its sources; where the plan's instinct differs from this brief, follow the brief and note it.
origin/main:docs/issues/820/byok-research-brief.md:44:- **Hard truth:** a tenant's own untrusted code can always read a key legitimately handed to it. Injection secures against OTHER tenants and the host — **per-workspace isolation is the real boundary.** Inject only the requesting tenant's own keys, one execution at a time. For **first-party trusted tools** (Tavily/transcription proxies), DO NOT inject — resolve host-side, make the outbound call from a trusted proxy, return only results to the sandbox.
